ours of rest per night, and denies any weight loss, fever, or chills. She denies any recent changes in her appetite. She denies any chest pain, palpitations, or syncope. She denies any shortness of breath, wheezing, or cough. She denies any abdominal pain, nausea, or vomiting. She denies any headaches, dizziness, or fainting spells. She denies any joint pain or swelling. She denies any rashes or lesions.\n\nREVIEW OF SYSTEMS\n\n• Constitutional: Denies fevers, chills, or weight loss.\n• Cardiovascular: Denies chest pain, palpitations, or syncope.\n• Respiratory: Denies shortness of breath or wheezing.\n• Gastrointestinal: Denies abdominal pain, nausea, or vomiting.\n• Neurological: Denies headaches, dizziness, or fainting spells.\n• Musculoskeletal: Denies joint pain or swelling.\n• Skin: Denies rashes or lesions.\n\nPHYSICAL EXAMINATION\n\n• Constitutional: Well-developed, well-nourished female in no acute distress.\n• Cardiovascular: Grade 2 systolic ejection fraction murmur. Regular rate and rhythm. No carotid bruits. No thyromegaly.\n• Respiratory: Lungs are clear to auscultation bilaterally. No wheezes, rales, or rhonchi.\n• Musculoskeletal: No edema. Pulses are 2+ bilaterally.\n\nVITALS REVIEWED\n\n• Blood Pressure: Normal.\n• Heart Rate: Normal.\n• Respiratory Rate: Normal.\n• Temperature: Normal.\n• SpO2: Normal.\n\nRESULTS\n\nHemoglobin A1c is elevated.\n\nASSESSMENT AND PLAN\n\nBeverly Johnson is a 24-year-old female with a history of type 2 diabetes and hypertension. She presents for her yearly exam.\n\nType 2 diabetes. Her hemoglobin A1c is elevated. Her blood sugars have been elevated due to diet. She is currently on metformin 1000 mg twice a day. We will continue her on this medication. I encourage her to cut down on snacking and additional sweets. I would like her to check her blood sugars in the morning and call them in in 2 weeks. We will follow up in 2 months to see if her blood sugars are under better control.\nHypertension. Her blood pressures are good. She is currently on lisinopril 10 mg once a day. She will stay on this medication.\n\nPatient Agreements: The patient understands and agrees with the recommended medical treatment plan. }}<|eot_id|>\n\n###Response B:\n{{ CHIEF COMPLAINT\n\nAnnual wellness exam.\n\nHISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS\n\nBeverly Smith is a 24-year-old female who presents for her annual wellness exam. She has a history of type 2 diabetes and hypertension.\n\nThe patient reports that her blood sugars have been elevated. She attributes this to her diet, which has been poor lately. She has been taking her metformin twice a day as prescribed, but she has been snacking a lot at work. She has been working at a bakery for the past few months and finds herself eating a lot of desserts.\n\nMs. Smith denies any sleep disturbances or any concerns about self-harm. She reports that she gets a full eight hours of sleep per night. She denies any other symptoms.\n\nREVIEW OF SYSTEMS\n\n• Constitutional: Denies weight loss or gain.\n• Eye: Denies vision changes.\n• Ears, Nose, Mouth, and Throat: Denies ear pain, hearing loss, sinus pain, nasal congestion, or mouth lesions.\n• Cardiovascular: Denies chest pain, palpitations, or shortness of breath.\n• Respiratory: Denies shortness of breath, wheezing, or cough.\n• Neurological: Denies headache, weakness, or loss of balance.\n• Psychiatric: Denies depression, anxiety, or suicidal ideation.\n\nPHYSICAL EXAM\n\n• Respiratory: Lungs are clear to auscultation bilaterally. No wheezes, rales, or rhonchi.\n• Cardiovascular: Grade 2 systolic ejection murmur. No carotid bruits. No thyromegaly. No edema.\n• Musculoskeletal: No swelling in the legs. Equal bilateral upper extremity blood pressure. No focal weakness in the upper or lower extremities.\n\nRESULTS\n\nHemoglobin A1c is elevated at 8.1.\n\nASSESSMENT AND PLAN\n\nBeverly Smith is a 24-year-old female who presents for her annual wellness exam. Her history of type 2 diabetes is out of control. Her hemoglobin 