 i see you\'re here for back pain .\n[patient] yes .\n[doctor] hey dragon , i\'m seeing mrs. lee today for , uh , back pain . she\'s a 40-year-old female . so can you tell me what happened to you ?\n[patient] yes . i was at a birthday party and it was surprise party . so we were all like crunched behind and hiding . and when the person came in we all jumped out , and then i fell down the little stair and i rammed my back into the railing of the s- , uh , staircase .\n[doctor] so it was a surprise for you too ?\n[patient] yes it was .\n[doctor] very good . okay . so , um , did it hurt because you hit it , or did you twist ? what happened when you ?\n[patient] i think it was my motion-\n[doctor] okay .\n[patient] . of jumping up , or something .\n[doctor] okay .\n[patient] and maybe a twisting .\n[doctor] okay . any pain shooting down your legs at all ?\n[patient] no .\n[doctor] is it on one side or the other of your back ?\n[patient] um , it\'s more on the right .\n[doctor] on the right . is there anything that makes it better or worse ?\n[patient] uh , if i\'m lying flat it hurts a little more , and any twisting motion for sure .\n[doctor] okay . all right . and do you have any history or any back problems before ?\n[patient] i did . i had a fusion done .\n[doctor] okay , yeah that lumbar fusion . um , let\'s take an x-ray . hey . or let\'s look at an x-ray . hey dragon , show me the latest x-ray . okay , we can see where you had your fusion , t4 here on the x-ray . but i do n\'t see anything that looks , um , significantly abnormal . it does n\'t appear to have any compression fractures or anything like that . so , it\'s essentially , except for the fusion , a normal exam . let me do your exam for you here . can i have you stand up please ? does it hurt when i twist you that way ?\n[patient] yes .\n[doctor] okay . you can sit back down . if i lift your leg here , does that bother you ?\n[patient] no .\n[doctor] how \'bout when i lift this leg ?\n[patient] no .\n[doctor] any pain shooting down your legs when i do that ?\n[patient] no .\n[doctor] okay . and can you feel me touching down here .\n[patient] yes .\n[doctor] okay , great . so , you had a injury to your back when you were twisting at the birthday party . you do n\'t really have any pain or numbness down your legs . you have some stiffness with rotation , um , but you do n\'t have any evidence of a , um , what we would call a pinched nerved or radiculopathy . so , i think you basically have a strain of your lower back . um , have you been taking any medications ?\n[patient] just some ibuprofen .\n[doctor] okay . and do you have any other , um , medical problems ?\n[patient] um i take medication for anxiety .\n[doctor] okay . and that\'s all ? okay . um , so what we\'re gon na do is i\'m gon na give you , um , just continue the motrin at 800mg , three times a day , with food . i want you to use some ice and heat , alternating on your back . but i do want you walking .\n[patient] okay .\n[doctor] um , and , uh , this should sort of take care of itself after a few days . if it\'s not , or it\'s getting worse , i want you to come back and see me over the next week .\n[patient] okay .\n[doctor] does that sound good ?\n[patient] it does .\n[doctor] okay , dragon . hey dragon , go ahead and order the medications and procedures and close out the note . come on i\'ll bring you out to check out .\n[patient] thank you .\n#############################\n\n\n###Response A:\n{{ CHIEF COMPLAINT\n\nBack pain.\n\nHISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS\n\nMrs. Lee is a 40-year-old female who presents today for back pain. She states she was at a birthday party and was surprised. She was hiding behind some people, and when the person came in, she jumped up and fell down the stairs and hit her back on the railing. She denies any pain radiating down her legs. She states the pain is more on the right side. She states that lying flat hurts a little more and any twisting motions definitely hurt.\n\nThe patient has a history of lumbar fusion. X-rays of the lumbar spine were obtained today. There is no evidence of compression fractures or anything significantly abnormal. The patient is otherwise normal.\n\nEXAM\n\nMSK: Examination of the back reveals some tenderness with rotation. No radiculopathy.\n\nRESULTS\n\nX-rays of the lumbar spine are reviewed today. There is no evidence of compression fractures or anything significantly abnormal. The patient has a history of lumbar fusion.\n\nASSESSMENT\n\nLumbosacral strain.\n\nPLAN\n\nAt this point, I have discussed the diagnosis with the patient. I have recommended that we treat her with Motrin, 800 mg, 3 times a day, with food. I have recommended that she use ice and heat alternating. I have recommended that she walk. She is to follow up in one week if her symptoms do not improve or worsen.\n\nINSTRUCTIONS\n\nShe will follow up in one week if her symptoms do not improve or worsen.\n }}<|eot_id|>\n\n###Response B:\n{{ CHIEF COMPLAINT\n\nBack pain.\n\nHISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS\n\nThe patient is a 40-year-old female who presents today for evaluation of back pain. The patient states that she was at a birthday party and they were all like crunched behind and hiding. When the person came in, we all jumped out, and she fell down the little stair and rammed her back into the railing of the staircase. She states that it was a surprise for her as well. She denies any pain shooting down her legs. She states that it hurts more when she is lying flat and any twisting motion for sure. The patient has a history of lumbar fusion. She denies any numbness or tingling.\n\nPHYSICAL EXAM\n\nMSK: Back: There is some stiffness with rotation. No pain with leg lift or straight leg raise. No focal tenderness.\n\nRESULTS\n\nX-ray images of the lumbar spine obtained today show no acute abnormalities. There is no compression fracture. The patient has a lumbar fusion.\n\nASSESSMENT\n\nLumbar strain.\n\nPLAN\n\nI have reviewed and discussed my assessment and plan with the patient. We discussed that she has a lumbar strain. I have recommended that she continue taking Motrin 800 mg 3 times a day with food. I have also recommended that she use ice and heat, alternating on her back. I want her to continue to walk. If her symptoms worsen or do not improve over the next week, she should return for reevaluation.\n\nINSTRUCTIONS\n\nThe patient will follow up with me in 1 week if her symptoms worsen or do not improve.\n }}<|eot_id|>\n\n###Reference Answer:\nCC:\n\nBack pain.\n\nHPI:\n\nMs. Lee is a 40-year-old female who presents today for an evaluation of back pain. She states she was at a birthday party and she ran her back into the railing of the staircase and twisted her back. She denies any pain radiating down her legs. Lying flat and twisting motions aggravate the pain. She is currently taking ibuprofen.\n\nCURRENT MEDICATIONS:\n\nIbuprofen.\n\nPAST MEDICAL HISTORY:\n\nAnxiety.\n\nPAST SURGICAL HISTORY:\n\nLumbar fusion\n\nEXAM\n\nExamination of the back shows range of motion without pain. Straight leg raise is negative. Sensation is intact.\n\nRESULTS\n\nX-rays of the lumbar spine show no obvious signs of acute fracture. Evidence of a prior lumbar fusion with hardware in good position.\n\nIMPRESSION\n\nLumbar spine strain.\n\nPLAN\n\nAt this point, I discussed the diagnosis and treatment options with the patient. I have recommended ice and heat. She will continue with Motrin 800 mg 3 times a day with food. She will follow up with me as needed.\n\n\n###Score Rubric:\n\n1. Completeness:\n    - Does the summary include all significant components of the SOAP format?\n    - Are there any crucial aspects of the conversation missing from the summary?\n2. Accuracy:\n    - How accurately does the summary reflect the details of the conversation as they were discussed?\n    - Are there any discrepancies between what was said and what is noted?\n3. Clarity and Structure:\n    - Is the summary well-organized, following the logical flow of Subjective, Objective, Assessment, Plan?\n    - Is the information presented in a clear and understandable manner?\n4. Use of Medical Terminology:\n    - Is medical terminology used correctly and effectively throughout the summary?\n    - Does the use of terminology enhance the clarity and precision of the summary?\n5. Evidence-Based Support:\n    - In the Assessment and Plan sections, are the conclusions and recommendations supported by appropriate medical guidelines or literature?\n    - Does the summary demonstrate a thoughtful and knowledgeable approach to patient care?\n\n\n###Feedback: '}], request_timeout=60)[0m


