ng activity. The pain is intermittent and occurs after swimming. She notes that she is able to swim 10 laps per month, but after 2 laps, she begins to feel pain. The pain is rated as 8 out of 10 in severity. She has been avoiding running and has limited her walking. She denies any swelling or redness.\n\nMEDICAL HISTORY\n\nThe patient reports a history of carpal tunnel release, depression, and chronic back pain.\n\nSOCIAL HISTORY\n\nThe patient reports that she is an administrative assistant. She is currently taking swimming lessons. She reports that she sleeps well, eats well, and denies that she has any suicidal or homicidal ideations. She reports that she is currently participating in therapy for depression.\n\nREVIEW OF SYSTEMS\n\nMusculoskeletal: Reports right knee pain. Denies left knee pain.\nPsychiatric: Reports depression. Denies suicidal or homicidal ideations.\n\nPHYSICAL EXAM\n\nMSK: Examination of the right knee: Pain with patellar inhibition. Pain with palpation of the patella. No edema.\nCV: Regular rate and rhythm. No murmurs, gallops, or rubs.\nRESPIRATORY: Lungs are clear to auscultation bilaterally. No wheezes, rales, or rhonchi. No adenopathy.\nNEURO: Strength is 5/5 bilaterally. No focal deficits.\n\nRESULTS\n\nX-ray of the right knee was reviewed and is unremarkable.\n\nASSESSMENT\n\nChondromalacia of the patella.\n• Chronic back pain.\n• Depression.\n• History of carpal tunnel release.\n\nPLAN\n\nFor her right knee pain, I have recommended that she take ibuprofen 600 mg 4 times a day with food. I have also recommended that she perform straight leg raising exercises. I advised her to avoid swimming breaststroke for 2 weeks.\n\nFor her chronic back pain, I have recommended that she continue to engage in swimming.\n\nFor her depression, I have recommended that she continue her current medication.\n\nFor her carpal tunnel syndrome, I have recommended that she continue to avoid activities that exacerbate her symptoms.\n\nINSTRUCTIONS\n\nThe patient will follow up in 2 weeks if her symptoms do not improve. }}<|eot_id|>\n\n###Response B:\n{{ CHIEF COMPLAINT\n\nRight knee pain.\n\nHISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS\n\nRebecca Johnson is a 27-year-old female who presents to the clinic today for the evaluation of right knee pain. The patient has a history of carpal tunnel release, chronic back pain, and depression. The onset of her right knee pain began over the last month. The patient has been swimming more regularly. She uses a breaststroke when swimming. The patient denies any injury to her right knee. The patient reports that she can do up to 10 laps without pain. However, after 2 laps of swimming, she starts to feel pain in her right knee. She denies any leg pain when walking. The patient denies any leg pain when walking. She has a history of chronic back pain, which she states is doing well. She has been taking medication for depression and has been seeing a therapist. She reports that her depression is stable. She denies any suicidal or homicidal ideation. The patient reports that her back pain is stable. She denies any leg swelling. She denies any numbness or tingling in her legs. She reports that her carpal tunnel release is doing well.\n\nREVIEW OF SYSTEMS\n\n• Constitutional: Denies fevers, chills, weight loss, or weight gain.\n• Musculoskeletal: Denies leg pain with ambulation. Denies lower extremity numbness or tingling.\n• Neurological: Denies lower extremity numbness or tingling. Denies weakness.\n• Psychiatric: Denies suicidal or homicidal ideation.\n\nPHYSICAL EXAM\n\n• Musculoskeletal: Examination of the right knee: Pain with patellar inhibition. No swelling.\n\nRESULTS\n\nX-rays of the right knee are normal.\n\nASSESSMENT AND PLAN\n\nChondromalacia of the patella.\n• Medical Reasoning: The patient presents today with right knee pain. Her history of swimming has likely led to overuse and irritation of the patella.\n• Patient Education and Counseling: I advised the patient to avoid breaststroke while swimming. We discussed the importance of rest and taking ibuprofen 600 mg 4 times a day with food.\n• Medical Treatment: I advised the patient to continue taking ibuprofen 600 mg 4 times a day with food. I also advised the patient to do straight leg raising exercises to strengthen the quadriceps. I advised the patient to avoid lunges and any other exercises that put extra pressure on the knees.\n• Additional Testing: We will re-evaluate the patient in 2 weeks if her symptoms do not improve.\n• Patient Agreements: The patient understands and agrees with the recommended medical treatment plan.\n\nChronic back pain.\n• Medical Reasoning: The patient reports that her back pain is stable.\n• Medical Treatment: No changes to her current treatment regimen are recommended at this time.\n\nDepression.\n• Medical Reasoning: The patient reports that her depression is stable.\n• Medical Treatment: No changes to her current treatment regimen are recommended at this time.\n\nCarpal tunnel release.\n• Medical Reasoning: The patient reports that her carpal tunnel release is stable.\n• Medical Treatment: No changes to her current treatment regimen are recommended at this time.\n\nPatient Agreements: The patient understands and agrees with the recommended medical treatment plan. }}<|eot_id|>\n\n###Reference Answer:\nCHIEF COMPLAINT\n\nJoint pain\n\nHISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS\n\nMs. Thompson is a 27 y.o. female with a history of carpal tunnel release done a couple years ago, chronic back pain, and depression. She presents today for joint pain.\n\nMs. Thompson reports that over the course of the month she has begun swimming more regularly and trying to be more active. She has experienced bad right knee pain. The patient reports that her knee starts hurting after a couple of laps. She notes previously she could swim 10 laps. She denies injury or trauma to the knee.\n\nThe patient has a history of chronic back pain that has been intermittent, but right now it has been doing well. She denies 